% ============================================================================
% morse-code-standard — the code system Morse code's letter/pattern table is
% built from → the international standard that specifies it, decoded from a
% span already sitting unused inside the SAME source `morse-code.adj` already
% cites (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `morse-code.adj` (`morse_code(letter,
% pattern)`, the 26 letter→dot/dash mappings): a new `morse_code_standard
% (code_system, standard_id)` table names the international standard that
% fixes those codes, decoded from a clause already sitting unused inside that
% table's own `source` field -- no new WebFetch. That table's schema has room
% for only a letter and its pattern; the already-quoted sentence also names
% the standard document itself, which never made it into a row:
%
%   international_morse_code → itu_r_m_1677_1
%     "The Morse code, as specified in the current international standard,
%      International Morse Code Recommendation, ITU-R M.1677-1, was derived
%      from a much-improved proposal by Friedrich Gerke in 1848."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? morse_code_standard(international_morse_code, $StandardId)  % itu_r_m_1677_1
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): the single `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `morse_code_standard(code_system, standard_id)` carrying the citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the engine returns
% the standard ID WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on any code system that is not the one tabled here (it never
% invents a standard ID). The query also runs BACKWARD -- bind a standard ID
% and recall which code system it specifies:
%
%     ? morse_code_standard($CodeSystem, itu_r_m_1677_1)  % international_morse_code
%
% This is honestly a ONE-ROW table: the already-quoted sentence names exactly
% one code system (International Morse code) and its one governing standard
% -- there is no second code system in this stdlib's already-cited material
% to compare it against. A recall on a different, real code system this
% quote does NOT name -- `american_morse_code`, a historically distinct
% Morse variant used on US telegraph lines before the international standard
% -- correctly abstains rather than inventing a standard ID for it.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME span already quoted inside
% `morse-code.adj`'s own `source` field, from the SAME Wikipedia "Morse code"
% article that table already cites -- no new WebFetch, only a different
% schema decoding a part of that already-verified quote. `trust consensus`
% -- the same tier the parent table already carries (an encyclopedia
% reproducing the ITU chart is a secondary reference, not the primary ITU
% document itself).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table morse_code_standard {
    columns code_system, standard_id

    row (international_morse_code, itu_r_m_1677_1)

    source "The Morse code, as specified in the current international standard, International Morse Code Recommendation, ITU-R M.1677-1, was derived from a much-improved proposal by Friedrich Gerke in 1848."
    locator "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morse_code"
    trust consensus
}
